        {
          "type": "output_text",
          "annotations": [],
          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"occurrence_id\":\"hamsun-victoria-14724dfffdb48ab5\",\"core_classification\":{\"label_support\":{\"truth_conditional\":4,\"performative\":0,\"exclamatory_reflexive\":0,\"other\":0},\"confidence\":0.97,\"analysis\":\"The embedded written declaration “jeg har elsket Dem” presents Victoria’s enduring love for Johannes as true. Its retrospective perfect form and explicit aim of showing him how deeply she has loved him make it a strong truth-conditional avowal. The letter seeks posthumous disclosure and emotional closeness, but the core utterance neither undertakes a relational commitment nor operates as a reflex-like discharge.\",\"ambiguity\":null},\"other_diagnosis\":{\"tpe_failure\":null,\"core_not_context\":null},\"utterance_status\":{\"status\":\"written\",\"description\":\"Victoria writes the avowal in a letter intended to be delivered to Johannes after her death; Johannes subsequently reads it.\"},\"contextual_interpretation\":\"Facing death, Victoria regrets having concealed her love and uses the letter to disclose it after ordinary shame and restraint have lost their force. The surrounding language is intensely emotional and includes fantasies of kissing Johannes’s shoes, his footprints, and the heather, but it is also sustained and reflective: she has lain in bed thinking about their last encounter and repeatedly worked on the letter. That intensity supports the depth of the avowed state, not an independently reflexive E force. Her wish to feel near Johannes through his reading explains the letter’s interpersonal purpose without amounting to a commitment or undertaking in the core declaration.\",\"evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"ev1\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“fik vist Dem hvor usigelig jeg har elsket Dem” (“show you how inexpressibly I have loved you”)\",\"supports\":\"The explicit perfect-form avowal presents Victoria’s profound, temporally extended love as true, strongly supporting T.\",\"confidence\":0.99},{\"evidence_id\":\"ev2\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“Jeg har ligget her på sengen og tænkt over de siste ord jeg sa til Dem”\",\"supports\":\"Her declaration follows prolonged reflection on her last words, favoring a considered avowal rather than a reflex-like expressive discharge.\",\"confidence\":0.97},{\"evidence_id\":\"ev3\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“Men nu når jeg skal dø og altsammen er forsent da skriver jeg til Dem endnu en gang og sier Dem det.”\",\"supports\":\"She explicitly characterizes the letter as telling Johannes of her love when everything is already too late, supporting disclosure rather than a new relational undertaking.\",\"confidence\":0.98},{\"evidence_id\":\"ev4\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“jeg vilde gjærne være nær hos Dem til det siste” and her imagining Johannes holding and reading the letter\",\"supports\":\"The letter seeks symbolic proximity and relief from abandonment, explaining its interpersonal function without independently establishing performative commitment.\",\"confidence\":0.94},{\"evidence_id\":\"ev5\",\"source\":\"supplied_metadata\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"The occurrence is marked as affirmative perfect (“perfect” / “no_perfect”) and located near the end of Knut Hamsun’s Victoria.\",\"supports\":\"Corroborates the retrospective grammatical framing and late-work death-letter setting of the avowal.\",\"confidence\":0.98}],\"background_knowledge\":{\"used\":false,\"familiarity\":\"none\",\"confidence\":null,\"contribution\":null},\"ontology_assessment\":{\"fit\":\"natural\",\"diagnosis\":\"T naturally captures the core as a retrospective written avowal. Its emotional intensity, posthumous delivery, and desire for closeness are adequately treated as contextual features; they do not require E, P, or O.\",\"candidate_recurrent_dimension\":null},\"notes\":null}"
        }
